{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-277862",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The roots of $g(x)$ are the solutions to the equation $g(x)=0.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-16}{{x}^{2}-7x+10}=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor $g(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-16}{{x}^{2}-7x+10} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+4)(x-4)}{(x-2)(x-5)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the numerator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)(x-4) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to $g(x)=0$, in ascending order, are $x=-4$ and $x=4.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
